ings not you know is every line  of this thing gospel and completely up  to date and so for that reason I do  recommend go one addition two additions  maybe three additions old but then I'm  starting to get a little bit  uncomfortable but one or two additions  old you're going to  pay a fraction of the cost you're going  to get a an enormous percentage of the  benefit from it and you're probably not  gonna be missing out on too much so yeah  I do think one of the big benefits of  textbooks is just the the degree to  which they are comprehensive so like  they're they're broad but not super deep  but that's totally fine because because  like Eric was getting that there's I  think it's underappreciated how  interconnected all of physiology is and  so if you're if you're like us and  you're you know into power lifting or  bodybuilding and you know mainly just  want to get jacked and or strong if you  open a physiology textbook you know  you're probably just going to jump  you're probably gonna look at the index  and see what is the skeletal muscle  chapter and then jump straight to that  but everything is so incredibly  interconnected so if you don't know  about metabolism then it's like oh we're  you know we're oxidizing carbohydrate  and fat but how is that stuff actually  getting delivered to the muscle and how  does that delivery work during exercise  and how is it regulated you may not care  at all about learning about the kidneys  but that's where most of the electrolyte  regulation goes on and so when you learn  about excitation contraction coupling  and how sodium like the sodium and  potassium gradients are super important  for depolarization of the sarcolemma  like that stuff working properly is  largely down to the kidneys when you  learn about how acidosis can affect  fatigue of the muscles that's going to  be regulated by both the kidneys and the  lungs so it's worth knowing how that  works and so you know maybe if you have  a little bit of pulmonary edema 